=== COMPILER2 DIAGNOSTICS ===
  [hir] E0010

  × invalid top-level `let`: a global variable binds a single name; destructuring and `_` are only available inside a body
    ╭─[declarations.baml:12:1]
 12 │ let [FIRST, SECOND] = [1, 2];
    · ──────────────┬──────────────
    ·               ╰── a global variable binds one name: `let NAME = value;` or `let NAME: Type = value;`
    ╰────

  [hir] E0010

  × invalid top-level `let`: a global variable binds a single name; destructuring and `_` are only available inside a body
    ╭─[declarations.baml:15:1]
 15 │ let _ = 3;
    · ─────┬────
    ·      ╰── a global variable binds one name: `let NAME = value;` or `let NAME: Type = value;`
    ╰────

  [hir] E0010

  × invalid top-level `let`: only a type annotation may follow a global variable's name
    ╭─[declarations.baml:18:1]
 18 │ let ALIAS: let OTHER = 4;
    · ────────────┬────────────
    ·             ╰── a global variable binds one name: `let NAME = value;` or `let NAME: Type = value;`
    ╰────

  [hir] E0010

  × invalid top-level `let`: `let … else` needs a body to leave; a global variable's initializer must produce its value
    ╭─[declarations.baml:21:1]
 21 │ let MAYBE: int = 5 else { 6 };
    · ───────────────┬──────────────
    ·                ╰── a global variable binds one name: `let NAME = value;` or `let NAME: Type = value;`
    ╰────

  [hir] E0176

  × `baml` cannot be used as a binding name: syntax such as `spawn` and `env.X` refers to the `baml` package, which this binding would hide. A class or function may still be named `baml`.
    ╭─[declarations.baml:24:5]
 24 │ let baml = 7;
    ·     ──┬─
    ·       ╰── reserved binding name
    ╰────

  [validation] E0011

  × name `Duplicated` defined 2 times as: let, function
    ╭─[declarations.baml:27:5]
 27 │ let Duplicated = 8;
    ·     ─────┬────
    ·          ╰── first defined as let here
    ╰────
    ╭─[declarations.baml:29:10]
 29 │ function Duplicated() -> int {
    ·          ─────┬────
    ·               ╰── duplicate function definition
    ╰────

  [type] E0002

  × unresolved type: NoSuchType
    ╭─[declarations.baml:34:17]
 34 │ let UNRESOLVED: NoSuchType = 10;
    ·                 ──────────
    ╰────

  [hir] E0147

  × the `_` wildcard type can only be inferred in a `let` binding or a `throws` clause, not in a global variable's type
    ╭─[declarations.baml:35:11]
 35 │ let HOLE: map<string, _> = { "eleven": 11 };
    ·           ───────┬──────
    ·                  ╰── `_` cannot be inferred here
    ╰────

  [hir] E0110

  × `void` can only be used as a function return type, not as a let binding annotation
    ╭─[declarations.baml:36:14]
 36 │ let NOTHING: void = null;
    ·              ──┬─
    ·                ╰── `void` not allowed here
    ╰────

  [type] E0001

  × mismatched types
    ╭─[declarations.baml:39:26]
 39 │ let MISMATCHED: string = 12;
    ·                          ─┬
    ·                           ╰── expected `string`, found `12`
    ╰────

  [hir] E0010

  ⚠ `const` is treated like `let` here: every global variable is already immutable, so declare it with `let`.
    ╭─[declarations.baml:42:1]
 42 │ const CONSTANT = 13;
    · ──┬──
    ·   ╰── `const` behaves like `let` for globals
    ╰────
